    "question": "Which of the following statements about a tree (an undirected graph) is always true?",
    "options": {
      "A": "A tree with n vertices has exactly n edges.",
      "B": "Removing any edge from a tree disconnects the graph.",
      "C": "A tree must contain at least one cycle.",
      "D": "Every tree is a complete graph."
    },
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "explanation": "In a tree, there is exactly one simple path between any two vertices; removing any edge destroys that path and therefore disconnects the graph. The other statements are false: a tree has n – 1 edges, contains no cycles, and is rarely complete (only for n = 1).",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "In a directed graph, a strongly connected component (SCC) is defined as a maximal set of vertices such that:",
    "options": {
      "A": "There is a directed path from each vertex to every other vertex in the set.",
      "B": "There is an undirected path between each pair of vertices in the set.",
      "C": "All vertices have the same in‑degree.",
      "D": "The subgraph induced by the set contains no cycles."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "An SCC is a maximal subset of vertices where for any two vertices u and v in the subset, there exists a directed path u → v and a directed path v → u. Options B, C, and D do not capture the directed‑reachability requirement.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Let G be a simple undirected graph with n ≥ 2 vertices. If G has more than ½ (n‑1)(n‑2) edges, which property must G satisfy?",
    "options": {
      "A": "G is bipartite.",
      "B": "G contains a Hamiltonian cycle.",
      "C": "G is necessarily connected.",
      "D": "G has a vertex of degree 1."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "The maximum number of edges a disconnected simple graph can have is achieved by a complete graph on n‑1 vertices plus an isolated vertex, which is ½ (n‑1)(n‑2) edges. Exceeding this bound forces the graph to be connected. The other properties are not guaranteed.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Dijkstra's algorithm may produce incorrect shortest‑path distances when applied to a weighted graph because:",
    "options": {
      "A": "The graph contains parallel edges.",
      "B": "The graph is directed.",
      "C": "Some edge weights are negative.",
      "D": "The graph has more than one connected component."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Dijkstra's algorithm assumes all edge weights are non‑negative; a negative weight can cause the algorithm to settle on a suboptimal distance. Parallel edges, direction, or multiple components do not inherently break the algorithm (provided non‑negative weights).",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following graph invariants is always preserved under graph isomorphism?",
    "options": {
      "A": "The degree sequence of the vertices.",
      "B": "The chromatic number of the graph.",
      "C": "Whether the graph is planar.",
      "D": "All of the above."
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "An isomorphism is a relabeling of vertices that keeps adjacency unchanged, so any property that depends solely on the abstract structure—degree sequence, chromatic number, planarity, etc.—remains identical. Hence all listed invariants are preserved.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  }
]
